{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed581286445/6/codegen_prompts/3.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed581286445/6`\n- 题号：`3`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed581286445/6/3.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"3\">\n- `description`：将网格中分散的彩色矩形边框按面积排序后，以公共中心从内到外嵌套绘制为同心框架。\n- `Input structure`：黑色背景的较大网格上散布着若干彩色像素簇。每种颜色（如 red、green、blue、yellow）各出现一组像素，每组像素构成一个 4-连通区域，其形状恰好是一个单像素厚的完整矩形边框（但在输入中这些边框被随机平移到网格各处，互不重叠）。各矩形边框大小各不相同。\n- `Rule`：\n  - 识别每种颜色像素簇的形状，确认它是一个矩形边框，并测量其宽度和高度。\n  - 按矩形边框的面积（宽 x 高）从小到大排序。\n  - 在输出网格（大小恰好能容纳最大边框）中，以网格中心为公共中心，从内到外依次绘制这些矩形边框：面积最小的在最内层，面积最大的在最外层。\n  - 每个边框保持其原来的颜色。\n- `Key insight`：输入中的碎片并非随意形状，而是完整的矩形边框被\"散落\"在不同位置；解题者需要先识别出每组颜色构成的是矩形框架，然后发现输出将这些框架按大小嵌套重组为同心结构——涉及\"形状识别\"和\"按属性排序重组\"两层独立推理。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：eval/89565ca0, train/6cbe9eb8, train/985ae207, eval/8f215267, train/ba1aa698, eval/e3721c99, train/68bc2e87, train/db615bd4\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"89565ca0\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.4359\nmechanism=0.5867\nlexical=0.0136\ncode_feature=0.2857\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:closed_region, object:frame, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, relation:direction, relation:overlap\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/89565ca0.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/89565ca0.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nConvert each colored partitioned frame into a horizontal bar whose length is its number of enclosed regions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with several axis-aligned colored line drawings.\n- The line drawings are damaged by scattered pixels of one non-background noise color and by overlaps with other drawings.\n- Each real drawing is a rectangular partitioned frame enclosing one or more background regions.\n\nRule:\n- For each non-background color, reconstruct its horizontal and vertical wall segments from dense row/column evidence.\n- Count the 4-connected background regions fully enclosed by those reconstructed walls.\n- The color with no enclosed region is the noise/pad color.\n- Sort drawing colors by enclosed-region count; equal counts are ordered by which reconstructed frame covers the other.\n\nKey insight:\n- The scattered color is not an object; it only pads the histogram, while each true object's room count is stable after rectilinear wall reconstruction.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"colored partitioned frame line drawings; ignore scattered pad-color pixels\",\n  \"dst\": \"stack one horizontal bar per frame, ordered by count of enclosed background regions then color id\",\n  \"transform\": \"bar length = number of enclosed 4-connected background regions; pad right with the scattered color\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"height = number of frame colors, width = maximum enclosed-region count\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6cbe9eb8\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.4163\nmechanism=0.5493\nlexical=0.0142\ncode_feature=0.3333\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:frame, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/6cbe9eb8.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/6cbe9eb8.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRebuild the foreground rectangles as a largest-to-smallest lower-left nesting.\n\nInput structure\n- A periodic wallpaper background is overlaid by foreground colors that each form a rectangular frame or solid rectangle.\n- The largest foreground rectangle is the output frame; smaller foreground rectangles are the objects to nest inside it.\n- A frame may be partially hidden by a smaller rectangle, but its visible pixels still determine its bbox.\n\nRule\n- Ignore the wallpaper and crop the result to the largest foreground rectangle's bbox size.\n- Sort foreground rectangles by bbox area from largest to smallest.\n- Reconstruct each frame or solid rectangle, placing every next smaller rectangle in the lower-left of the current rectangle's usable region.\n\nKey insight\n- The scattered background colors are not objects; the meaningful order is the unique descending rectangle size chain, and each step nests into the previous rectangle's lower-left interior.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"foreground rectangular frames and solid rectangles over periodic wallpaper\",\n  \"dst\": \"nested lower-left inside the largest foreground rectangle\",\n  \"transform\": \"discard the wallpaper; sort rectangles by bbox area descending; rebuild each frame or solid rectangle, placing each next smaller rectangle at the lower-left of the current frame interior or solid bbox\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"crop to the largest foreground rectangle bbox\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"985ae207\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3838\nmechanism=0.5015\nlexical=0.0142\ncode_feature=0.3333\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:frame, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, relation:direction, relation:overlap\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/985ae207.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/985ae207.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRepeat each 3x3 control-center sprite toward the matching solid rectangle until a copy first overlaps that rectangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Each sprite is a 3x3 foreground object whose eight border pixels have one color and whose center has a different control color.\n- For every control color, there is exactly one separate solid rectangular object of that color.\n\nRule\n- Match each sprite to the unique solid rectangle whose color equals the sprite center.\n- Copy the whole 3x3 sprite in the cardinal direction of that rectangle, stepping by three cells each time.\n- Stop after pasting the first copy whose 3x3 bbox overlaps the rectangle; copied pixels overwrite the rectangle there.\n\nKey insight\nThe center pixel is not the paint color: it is a destination key, while the entire 3x3 sprite is the motif tiled along the straight path.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"3x3 sprite s with a uniform border color and a different control-color center\",\n  \"path\": \"straight cardinal path from s toward d\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"copy s every three cells until the first copied bbox overlaps d\",\n  \"dst\": \"separate solid rectangle d whose color matches s.center\",\n  \"transform\": \"copied sprites overwrite background/rectangle cells; all other input pixels remain unchanged\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8f215267\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3852\nmechanism=0.5116\nlexical=0.0150\ncode_feature=0.2857\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:frame, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:translate, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/8f215267.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/8f215267.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nCount same-color patterns per frame and represent the tally as dots inside.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Hollow rectangular frames in various colors.\n- Small monochrome pattern sprites scattered outside frames.\n\nRule:\n- Remove all small patterns from the grid.\n- For each frame of color C, count how many patterns share color C.\n- Draw that many dots along the frame's center row, spaced by 2, starting from the right.\n\nKey insight: Frames act as tally displays — scattered same-color patterns are \"absorbed\" and their count is shown as interior dots.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"pattern sprites s outside frame sprites\",\n  \"dst\": \"fill hollow rectangular frame sprites d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.color == d.color\",\n  \"transform\": \"for each frame d: N = #{s | s.color == d.color}; draw N dots on mid-row(d), spaced by 2 from the right side\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ba1aa698\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3766\nmechanism=0.4909\nlexical=0.0141\ncode_feature=0.3333\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:counter, object:frame, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/ba1aa698.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/ba1aa698.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nExtrapolate the moving sprite from a horizontal sequence of shared-border frames into the next standalone frame.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is a row of equal-width framed panels sharing their vertical border columns.\n- Each panel has the same fill color and one identical interior sprite at the same local columns.\n- The sprite's top row changes by a constant amount from panel to panel.\n\nRule\n- Build one output panel with the same height, frame color, fill color, and sprite.\n- Continue the sprite's diagonal motion one more panel to the right.\n- Because the output is a standalone panel, advance by the full panel width, not the shared-border spacing.\n\nKey insight\n- The panels are consecutive frames of motion; the output is the next frame after the sequence.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"identical interior sprite instances inside the shared-border framed panels\",\n  \"path\": \"constant diagonal motion from panel to panel, continued one more standalone panel to the right; vertical offset is scaled from shared-border spacing to full panel width and rounded to the nearest row\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"stamp one copy of the sprite in the next framed panel at the extrapolated row and the same local columns\",\n  \"transform\": \"discard the previous panels; draw only the new single frame with the same frame and fill colors\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"same height, width of one standalone panel\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e3721c99\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3528\nmechanism=0.4391\nlexical=0.0139\ncode_feature=0.4286\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:closed_region, object:frame, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:sort_order, operation:translate, relation:direction\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/e3721c99.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/e3721c99.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRecolor grey shapes by matching their hole count to a color legend.\n\nInput structure:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
